at's  a reality the point is when the right  c-span  as not worthwhile when they don't like  what we're spending money on they say  we've got to do some responsible  spending cuts and we've got to save some  money when the right wants the spending  because it's fueling an industry or a  sector that they like they call an  attempt to cut spending a job killer  it's a political football much like debt  and deficit I'll give you one more  example the coal industry the coal  industry should die coal has horrible  negative externalities pollution causes  illness hurts the planet it's  inefficient it's antiquated but if you  reduce the coal industry by 90 percent  you cut roughly ninety percent of those  jobs and that's okay sometimes  industries are obsolete we need to  figure out how everybody can keep making  a living they need to people are gonna  need to find other jobs but the argument  that we should just prop up coal so that  people don't lose coal jobs is just as  bad as saying we shouldn't do  medicare-for-all because some people  will lose jobs it's a political football  it's like debt and deficit we've talked  about it before so then it becomes a  question of given that every economic  policy affects jobs at a macroeconomic  level what is worth doing if we cut  education spending by 10 percent we're  cutting teachers by 10 percent this will  worsen education it will continue to  make us less competitive globally in  industry it'll generate more crime since  more educated people commit less crime  it is not good for the economy over the  long term if we cut Medicare if we cut  health care spending with something like  Medicare for all we eliminate medical  bankruptcies we put more money in  people's pockets to reinvest into the  economy by going to local businesses we  eliminate people keeping a job they hate  just because they need to keep that  health insurance plan we eliminate  people being sicker because they forego  earlier treatment to maybe avoid the  c